WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.989
Film Courage: What are some of the most important
questions you are asking yourself when you

00:00:03.989 --> 00:00:06.029
are developing a story idea?

00:00:06.029 --> 00:00:13.710
Jeffrey Reddick, Screenwriter: I think who
my audience is.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:14.879
Is the story interesting enough?

00:00:14.879 --> 00:00:21.369
Usually for my horror stuff, is there enough
stuff here to care about the characters, but

00:00:21.369 --> 00:00:26.730
are we coming up with enough scares that we
haven’t seen before?

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.849
Those are the kind of things I ask myself.

00:00:28.849 --> 00:00:33.700
It’s interesting because I love doing it
and I’ve done it for so long and it’s

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:38.470
not easy work at all because people don’t
understand especially when you are threading

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:43.080
a script together and dissecting it and making
sure everything kind of lines up and pieces

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.360
together, you want to put your best foot forward.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:50.830
But you also realize that once you sell and
it’s out of your hands, a lot of that stuff

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:53.240
just goes out of the window.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.030
So that’s why I’m not as harsh when I
see a movie.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:59.380
I reserve judgment on the writer until I read
the actual screenplay because you just never

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:02.300
know what changes from page to screen.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:08.400
But for me it’s usually (because it’s
always horror), also do I think this is going

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.210
to strike a chord with people.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:18.390
FINAL DESTINATION deals with death which is
kind of a universal fear which will reach

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:21.750
a wide audience (I’m not going to lie about
that).

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:25.420
You want to hit as many people as you can
with your concept.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.340
That’s what I love about the horror genre.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:35.590
I think the fears that we have are a universal
fear that aren’t regulated to one region

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:37.701
or one part of the world.

00:01:37.701 --> 00:01:42.979
I think we all kind of have the same fears
deep down so I think you can kind of tackle

00:01:42.979 --> 00:01:47.250
a whole bunch of different kinds of stories
and still have it play well all around the

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:48.250
world.

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:50.940
Film Courage: I like how you use the analogy
of it’s like knitting.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:56.830
And if you, let’s say you are knitting or
crocheting something and you pull one little

00:01:56.830 --> 00:02:01.870
string here, that it can really effect the
whole sweater or whatever?

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:04.200
Jeffrey Reddick: Yeah and a lot of people
don’t realize that especially when they

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:10.280
give you notes…like I know one time I had
a project where the producers came in and

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:12.980
they were like “We think he’s full of
sh*t but we’re going to give him a shot

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.600
at directing,” because he directed some
things before.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:21.800
But his pitch…he kind of came in…he had
this opening scene because this whole movie

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:25.510
built up around this plot twist about these
characters not knowing each other and his

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:29.610
opening scene was basically it was very clear
that these characters knew each other.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:33.880
And in my first meeting with him I was trying
to be very diplomatic.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:41.000
I was like “How do you propose we do that
opening scene so we don’t spoil the fact

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.890
that they know each other because that was
kind of the whole twist of the script and

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:47.290
it was one of those “Well, you’re the
writer.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:48.300
You figure it out.”

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:54.020
It’s kind of like, I will spoil the end
of USUAL SUSPECTS if you haven’t seen it.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:03:00.350
But that’s like going pretend Kevin Spacey
didn’t make it all up and Keyser Söze is

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:01.350
a real person.

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:04.910
But you are like “The whole point of the
movie is…”

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:10.430
Or like the end of the SIXTH SENSE, pretend
Bruce Willis like is a real person and not

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:18.530
a…But when they come to do something like
that, they obviously don’t understand, you

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:23.260
are taking the core of what makes this movie
like the core mystery and you are giving it

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:25.860
away at the beginning and so it’s going
to change the whole movie.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.319
And you’re like “Aahh, you’ll figure
it out.”

00:03:28.319 --> 00:03:36.310
So it shows a certain level of understanding
of how much thought you do need to put into

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:37.459
a good screenplay.

